# What survives on a small team's rig: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers with cheap hardware and little data

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted. SKELETON: each section below is a list of facts for the author to write in his own words; tables, figure and appendix are final.*

## Abstract

- Most robot-learning papers are tested in simulation or on rigs above $20k ({{rig_far}} of our {{papers}}).
- We re-tested {{c_paper}} of their claims on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}: an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX.
- For each claim, the bar is fixed before the test and the cheapest baseline is run first.
- {{c_paper_repro}} claims hold and {{c_paper_fail}} fail, in three ways: too slow for the edge GPU, judges that lose to episode length, latents that lose to the commanded joints.
- Many fails trace back to our small, short-task data as much as to the methods.
- Code, data and every number are public.

## 1. Introduction

- {{rig_far}} of {{papers}} papers shown in simulation or on a rig above $20k; {{rig_lt5k}} on a rig under $5k like ours.
- A small team has to guess, or re-test every claim itself, which is slow and costly.
- The question: does a claim hold for a small team, with cheap hardware, little data and short tasks?
- This paper: re-test the claims, one at a time, on one cheap rig.
- Example, adaptive chunking:
  - idea: choose the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks; long in transport, short at the grasp
  - one decision needs 20 sampled chunks → {{ex_a_ours}} on the Orin, budget {{ex_a_bar}} → fail
  - a fail on our rig, not on their hardware
- Overall: {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} hold, {{c_paper_fail}} fail.
- Several failures are not near misses: the method loses to a baseline with no learning → three classes, Section 5.

## 2. Rig and data

- Rig: SO-101 arm (5 joints + gripper), top and wrist cameras, Jetson Orin NX 16 GB for inference; training on rented cloud GPUs. Exact cost: €{{rig_eur}} for the priced parts ({{rig_unpriced}} small parts not priced yet), ${{cost}} of cloud GPU.
- Teleoperated demonstrations (`so101_bowls_all_trimmed`): {{tel_eps}} episodes, {{tel_frames}} frames at {{tel_fps}} fps (about {{tel_min}} minutes); two short bowl tasks (one bowl into another); split {{tel_train}} train / {{tel_val}} validation episodes. Used to train and test the world models and VLAs.
- Policy rollouts (`so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`): {{ro_eps}} episodes of our trained policies on the arm, {{ro_succ}} successes and {{ro_fail}} failures, labelled by the operator. Used for the failure monitors.
- ArmnetBench [@2607.24481]: {{arm_n}} public labelled SO-101 rollouts from {{arm_pol}} policies. Used for the reward models and judges.
- Timing: {{tim_eps}} recorded episodes, {{tim_b}} chunk boundaries. Used for chunking and latency.
- Limits of the data:
  - short tasks only, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode; no long-horizon task (several steps, memory) → we say nothing about long horizon
  - one task family, one arm, one room
  - outcome labels from one operator, not blind

## 3. What we tested and how

- Four kinds of papers, chosen from the stack our rig runs (Table 1):
  - world models ({{f_wm}}): learn a latent from the camera to predict the next state or plan; we test whether the latent predicts or plans better than a signal we get for free
  - failure monitors and judges ({{f_mon}}): say whether an episode fails or how far it has progressed; we test whether they catch failures better than a trivial signal
  - VLA policies ({{f_vla}}): follow an instruction on the arm; we test whether they succeed on our arm, over 10 trials judged by the operator
  - chunking and edge inference ({{f_chunk}}): how a policy's action chunks are produced and joined; we test smoother joins and the time per chunk on the Orin
- Rule 1: the bar is written from the paper and its code only, before any result; a bar written after the result drifts toward it.
- Rule 2: before the method, the cheapest baseline of its kind, on the same data (Table 1):
  - built from what we already have for free: the last frame, the joint values we send, the length of the episode, or the method with its trick switched off
  - if the method loses to it, the bar barely matters
- Verdicts: holds (bar met), fails (bar missed), not proven (test ran, cannot decide), not tested (planned).
- A fail can be our fault, so we run a control. Example, TurboVLA (our mistake):
  - we thought the architecture made our port of the policy ignore its instruction
  - bar: swapping the task moves the output by {{tv_bug}} → our port is the bug; by {{tv_data}} → the data
  - upstream's own trainer on our data: {{tv_swap}}; their released LIBERO checkpoint on our rig: {{tv_ctl}}
  - → our data, not their architecture; we withdrew the claim, and it is not counted here

Table 1: The four kinds of papers, what we test in each, the cheapest baseline we build for it, and the verdicts.

| kind of paper | papers | what we test | cheapest baseline | verdicts |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| world models | {{f_wm}} | predict the next state; rank or plan actions | copy-last (next state = current state); the commanded joints or the actions alone | {{f_wm_v}} |
| failure monitors and judges | {{f_mon}} | catch failures; track progress | episode length; the same model with shuffled actions | {{f_mon_v}} |
| VLA policies | {{f_vla}} | succeed on our arm | none cheaper: a success rate fixed before 10 trials | {{f_vla_v}} |
| chunking and edge inference | {{f_chunk}} | smoother joins between chunks; time per chunk | the same policy without the trick; the real-time budget on the Orin | {{f_chunk_v}} |

